course: dictionary
lesson: 9743
title: Sunstroke: a word for hot days
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sunstroke is an illness. You can get it if you stay in hot sun for too long. The words around it leave clues: headache, dizziness, a high body temperature, sickness and sometimes fainting all describe how a body feels when it is too hot.
explain: Sunstroke is a serious kind of heatstroke, so it is caused by the sun, not by a cold or a bug. Ask yourself what word would fit the sentence, then swap in your meaning and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Old words can hide in everyday speech. «A touch of the sun» means mild sunstroke, but it is old-fashioned and British. If a grown-up says it after a hot day, the clues tell you they feel a bit unwell from the heat. When in doubt, check a dictionary.
fact: Sunstroke is an illness caused by staying too long in hot sun.
fact: Signs of sunstroke include a headache, dizziness, a high body temperature, sickness and sometimes fainting.
fact: Sunstroke is a serious kind of heatstroke.
fact: «A touch of the sun» means mild sunstroke; it is old-fashioned and British.
fact: To prevent sunstroke, wear a hat, drink water and stay in the shade in the hottest part of the day.
exercise: «The children played outside all afternoon in the blazing sun, and Sam felt dizzy and sick.» What is Sam probably suffering from? | Hunger | *Sunstroke | A sprained ankle | Hay fever
exercise: What should you do to prevent sunstroke on a hot day? | Wear a thick woolly jumper | Stay in the sun for as long as you can | Skip drinks to save time | *Wear a hat and drink plenty of water
exercise: Which word names an illness caused by too much hot sun? | sunrise | *sunstroke | sunscreen | sundial
test: In first aid, what should you do for someone with sunstroke? | Give them a hot drink and a thick blanket | Leave them in the sun so they can rest | Make them run around to warm up | *Move them to a cool place, cool them down and get help
test: «A touch of the sun» means what? | A sunny day at the beach | A holiday abroad | A sunburnt nose only | *Mild sunstroke
exam: Australians use the reminder «Slip, slop, slap». What does it help children do? | Choose their dinner | *Protect themselves from the sun | Swim faster | Learn to whistle
